- We'll implement a Parser class that reads tokens from cin.
- OutputWriter can just use cout.
- MathUtils::pas function to compute Pascal's triangle modulo mod.
- Matrix class with multiplication and exponentiation.

Note: In Java, the matrix multiplication uses three nested loops. In C++, we can do the same. Since matrix size is 83, O(n^3) is fine.

We must be careful with modulo operations: use long long for intermediate products to avoid overflow (since mod is 1e9+7, product of two modded numbers fits in 64-bit: (1e9+7)^2 ~ 1e18 < 2^63-1).

The Java code uses (1L << (pot-coef)) % mod. Since pot <= 40, 1L << (pot-coef) fits in 64-bit. In C++, we can compute (1LL << (pot-coef)) % mod.

The matrix exponentiation is recursive in Java. We can implement it iteratively or recursively. Recursive is fine for log2(n) depth (n up to 1e18? Actually n is long, so up to 9e18, depth ~60). But recursive might cause stack overflow? In C++ recursion depth of 60 is fine. However, we can implement iterative exponentiation for safety. But the Java code uses recursive modpow. We'll keep recursive for simplicity.

